Starting intuititive - Think about how most levels in the
main dungeon are laid out.  Rooms appear, corridors
link the rooms.  Assume the pattern on this level is
typical, guess where other rooms are, guess where
the doors will be to the corridors.  Start your search
pattern at a corner of a room where you think there
will be a corridor.  Yes, the RNG delivers levels with
strange shapes, but going with the typical typically
works fine.

> If there seems to be an unusually wide black area at East or West, it is
> likely that there is a room behind a hidden corridor. A hidden door is
> probably in a room near that area. The screen has more width than height,
> and vertical walls are more likely to have hidden doors than horizontal
> ones.

Search first towards the center of the screen then towards
the edges - Most of the corridors that go anywhere are not
on the edge of the screen.  If you have a corridor do plenty
of searches at the end - There are more secret doors at the
ends of corridors that dead end corridors.

I don't do searches every two spaces.  Once I've made my
guesses where to expect corridors I move to a corner of that
wall, do "5s" then move one space and do another "5s".
Each search covers 3 squares on the wall so most squares
end up getting searched 15 times.  Very brute force.
Especially brute force if I kick each square before moving on.

At the end of a corridor I'll "5s", kick one direction, "5s", kick
the next direction, "5d", kick the third direction, then give up
figuring it's a dead end corridor.

If monsters come out of a corridor I search and kick along it
as well as at the end up it.  If a corridor makes a square
around an empty spot on the screen I'll search and kick
where I think the room is.

All of this is faster work using a pickaxe instead of searching.


